3. the same feeds, read from inside a contract
   through FeedAccessProbe at 0x1ddd6B597b6FF96f4bcA0CD57650cA53d1A42815

   HBAR / USD   decimals() ok    latestRoundData() REVERT 'No access'
   USDC / USD   decimals() ok    latestRoundData() REVERT 'No access'
   USDT / USD   decimals() ok    latestRoundData() REVERT 'No access'
   DAI / USD    decimals() ok    latestRoundData() REVERT 'No access'
   BTC / USD    decimals() ok    latestRoundData() REVERT 'No access'
   ETH / USD    decimals() ok    latestRoundData() REVERT 'No access'
   LINK / USD   decimals() ok    latestRoundData() REVERT 'No access'

   7 of 7 refuse a contract while answering its scale.

   This is the finding. An externally owned account reads a price and
   a contract does not, because the proxy's access controller admits a
   caller only when it is allowlisted or when msg.sender == tx.origin.
   eth_call cannot show it: eth_call sets tx.origin to whatever `from`
   says, so the two are equal again and the check passes. Every read in
   section 1 above is an eth_call and every one of them succeeded.

   decimals() is not gated, and that is the trap. PrimeOracle validates
   a feed by its scale before seating it, because the scale is the one
   thing that has to match. So the seat took, the deployment succeeded,
   and the first markToMarket reverted CashFeedStale. The venue behaved
   correctly throughout: a leg it cannot read is dark, and dark opens
   RepoVault.postMark. But the feed was never going to work.

   Hedera **mainnet**, same question, same probe bytecode:
     0x1B9a65b54e36A4f4E3591e4deAAb423C64959Ae0
       extcodesize        22337
       decimals()         ok, 8
       latestRoundData()  REVERT 'No access'

     Recorded rather than read live, because the probe contract is
     deployed on 296 only and putting one on mainnet would spend real
     HBAR to learn something a fork already answers. `forge script`
     runs against a fork of whatever RPC it is given, and a fork
     carries the target chain's contract bytecode, so Chainlink's
     access controller executes there exactly as it does on the
     network. Reproduce it in one command, free:

       FEED=0x1B9a65b54e36A4f4E3591e4deAAb423C64959Ae0 \
         forge script script/ProbeFeedAccess.s.sol:ForkProbe \
           --rpc-url https://mainnet.hashio.io/api

     A fork is the wrong instrument for `0x168` and for `0x16b`,
     which are the node's and are not bytecode. Those are read with
     `cast` against the real relay, which is what section 4 does.

==========================================================================
4. what the venue seats instead: Hedera's own exchange rate, 0x168
   tinybarsToTinycents(1e14)  806413333333333
   USD per HBAR, eight dp     8064133   = 0.08064133 USD
   Chainlink says             8254051   = 0.08254051 USD
   they differ by             2.30 percent

   Both are HBAR/USD and neither is wrong. Chainlink's is aggregated
   from exchanges; Hedera's is set by the council and is what every
   transaction fee on the network is priced at. The venue seats the
   second because it is the one a contract can read, through
   venue/src/oracle/HederaRateFeed.sol, which wears the Chainlink
   interface so the seat stays swappable. What replaces staleness as
   the risk on that seat is divergence from the market, which is the
   number printed directly above and which docs/RULEBOOK.md section
   4.2 states in words rather than pretending a heartbeat covers it.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------
every feed answers eight decimals:   True
   PrimeOracle._requireEightDecimals refuses anything else rather
   than normalising, and this is why that refusal is safe: there is
   no feed here it would turn away.

oldest answer across the seven:      68362s
registry heartbeat:                  86400s
DeployOracle.CASH_HEARTBEAT:         93600s
   Kept at the publisher's heartbeat plus slack even though the seat
   now holds an adapter that cannot go stale. It costs nothing, and
   it is the bound that binds again the moment a real Chainlink
   aggregator is seated on a chain that permits contract reads.

The other six feeds are read to establish that both claims above are
about Chainlink on this chain, and not about one address that happened
to behave that way.
